@h1 Additive partials h1@

An additive partial lets @strong many places contribute to one growing node
strong@. Prefix a definition with @@@code + code@@@ — @@@code +#name code@@@ —
and every declaration of that name @strong merges strong@ in document order.
This is how a table of contents, a bibliography, or a figure list assembles
itself as chapters are written and reordered.

@h2 The problem it solves h2@

Without additive partials, a bibliography is a second list you maintain by hand,
always drifting from the citations. With them, each chapter registers its own
entry where the citation lives, and the bibliography is simply the merge of all
of them.

@h2 The two shapes h2@

Both definition shapes take the @@@code + code@@@ prefix. Block:

@@@pre
+#toc
I. The Lamp
toc#

+#toc
II. The Watch
toc#

@toc
pre@@@

That merges to @code I. The Lamp II. The Watch code@ (verified). And
single-line:

@@@pre
+#bibliography: @weil Simone Weil, Gravity and Grace, 1952 !!
pre@@@

(fixtures 08-additive-partials/block-additive, single-line-additive.) A lone
@@@code +#name code@@@ with no sibling is a complete one-entry definition
(simple-additive-prefix). Additive defs may carry captures with
@@@code ::name:: code@@@ just like ordinary defs (additive-with-captures).

@h2 Order and mixing h2@

Contributions merge in @strong document order strong@, and across files in
reference (depth-first) order (fixtures multiple-additive-same-file,
order-preservation, cross-file-merge). A non-additive @strong base strong@ can
seed the node and additives extend it (mix-normal-and-additive).

@aside(class note) 💡 All contributions to one name must agree on @strong body shape strong@ — you cannot mix a block contribution with a single-line one. A mismatch is @code E_PARTIAL_SHAPE_MISMATCH code@ (fixture mixed-body-shape). aside@

@h2 Across files h2@

The pattern that makes a self-organising bibliography work: a master file
references each chapter and holds a bare @@@code @bibliography code@@@; each
chapter self-registers with @@@code +#bibliography code@@@.

@@@pre
~ master.wit
reference ./one.wit
reference ./two.wit

@bibliography

~ one.wit
+#bibliography: @weil Simone Weil, Gravity and Grace, 1952 !!
pre@@@

Every referenced file's contributions merge into the one node, in reference
order (fixture 08-additive-partials/cross-file-merge). The reference graph is
the scope.

@h2 Common mistakes h2@

@ul
@li Mixing body shapes across contributions (block vs single-line) —
@code E_PARTIAL_SHAPE_MISMATCH code@. li@
@li Declaring a plain @@@code #bibliography code@@@ in the master when chapters
already use @@@code +#bibliography code@@@ — unless you mean it as the base. li@
@li Putting whitespace between the @@@code + code@@@ and the @@@code # code@@@ —
write @@@code +#name code@@@. li@
ul@

